hey this is presh talwalkar  thanks to mustoba ali in bangladesh for  sending me this problem  two brothers a and b  have the exact same walking speed and  the exact same running speed  one day they started the same trip to  the same place  brother a  walked for half of the distance and ran  the rest  while brother b  walked for half of the time and ran for  the other half of time  which brother reached the end of the  trip first  can you figure it out  give this problem a try and when you're  ready keep watching the video for a  solution  [Music]  so there are many ways to solve this  problem and i'm going to present three  different methods  first i'll explain the answer using  logical reasoning then i'll go over a  method of using concrete numbers and  finally i'll give a formal algebraic  proof  so let's get started with logical  reasoning  brother a runs for half of the distance  so brother a must be running for less  than half of the time  this is because brother a also walks  half the distance and walking the same  distance takes more time than running  since brother b runs half the time  brother b is running for more time and  walking for less time so brother b will  be faster and complete the journey first  amazing we solve this without doing any  actual numerical calculation  but if that wasn't convincing to you you  could use concrete numbers and use your  poetic license to fill in distances and  speeds that were not specified in the  problem  so let's suppose brother a walks 500  miles and then runs 500 more just to be  the man to go 1000 miles to fall down at  your door  imagine both brothers walk at a rate of  5 miles per hour and run at a rate of 10  miles per hour  how long will brother a take  we take half the distance of 500 and  divide it by the walking speed of 5 then  we take the other half of 500 and divide  it by the running speed of 10 to get a  total of 150 hours  now brother b runs two times as fast so  he must run two times the distance to  have an equal running time and walking  time  so we can then calculate he must run for  one thousand times two-thirds miles and  he must walk for one thousand times one  third miles  we can then calculate brother b's total  time for the trip  this will be the walking distance of one  thousand over three divided by the  walking speed of five  plus the running distance of one  thousand times two  divided by the running speed of ten  this results in a total time of 133 and  one-third hours  so this is less time than brother a so  brother b completes the trip  first  now let's just go over a formal proof  suppose the total distance is d units  the walking speed is w units per hour  and the running speed is r units per  hour and suppose r is greater than w  the total time that brother a takes will  be half the distance divided by the  walking speed plus half the distance  divided by the running speed  we make a common denominator and this  simplifies to be the distance d times  the quantity r plus w all over the  quantity to rw  now to calculate the average speed of  brother a we take the total distance and  divide it by the total time  this simplifies to be the quantity 2rw  divided by the quantity r plus w  this is the harmonic mean of the two  numbers r and w  now brother b's calculation for average  speed is much easier his speed is a  simple arithmetic average because he's  running for half the time and walking  for half the time  so his average speed is the quantity r  plus w all over two  so which brother is faster  brother a has an average speed equal to  the harmonic mean of r and w while  brother b has an average speed equal to  the arithmetic mean of r and w  for two distinct positive numbers the  arithmetic mean is always larger this is  a well-known property and i've linked to  a proof of that in the video description  therefore brother b has a larger or  faster average speed so he will finish  the trip first  thanks for watching this video  i post math videos that you can watch  for free and i've so far stayed free  with no sponsors for very important  reasons  if you'd like to support the channel  here's how you can help  please subscribe for free to get the  newest videos  also watch and share all of mind your  decisions videos so that more people can  discover this channel  you can email me a math topic or puzzle  suggestion at presh  mindyourdecisions.com  if you like this video you can also  check out my books which are linked in  the video description and if you so  choose you can financially support this  channel on patreon  thanks for watching and thank you for  supporting this channel
 

